# How text reaches the object database, and how it comes back out.
#
# Three later checks compare bytes: a rendered page against a committed golden
# file, one build against another, and a formatter against a file's shape. All
# three are decided by what git wrote when somebody committed, so what it writes
# is declared here rather than left to the machine that ran the commit.

# Text is normalised to LF in both directions. The inward half stops a
# contributor on a system that ends lines with a carriage return from committing
# one, which would make a golden file differ from the page the build renders on
# every other machine, in a way that reads as a template change.
#
# The outward half is `eol=lf` and it is the half this file was missing. Left to
# the local system, a clone on a system that converts on checkout carries
# carriage returns in the working tree; gofmt writes LF, so the format leg
# refuses every Go file in a clone where nothing is wrong, and a template or a
# content file read by the build produces different bytes on one operating
# system than on another. `.editorconfig` already declares `end_of_line = lf`
# for every file here, so this is the same decision enforced on the way out
# rather than a second one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures whose bytes are the point are never normalised, in either direction.
# A golden file is compared byte for byte against what the build wrote, so a
# carriage return added on checkout would fail the comparison on one operating
# system and pass it on another, and a fixture git repaired on the way in proves
# the opposite of what it was written for.
*.golden        -text
testdata/**     -text

# Build output. It is excluded from language statistics and shown collapsed in a
# diff, where the change of interest is in the source that produced it rather
# than in the bytes it emitted.
dist/**         linguist-generated=true
